[Source "Daniel Naroditsky, London System Unveiled"]

1. d4 Nf6 2. Bf4 c5! {Repertoire headline: meet the London with immediate central contact. This is the combative choice Naroditsky recommends when you want London players out of their quiet structure. The goal is not sterile equality; it is a dynamic game you know better than White.}  (2... d5 {Playable, but more standard. If this is already your comfort zone, keep it; the lesson is to know one anti-London system deeply.}  )  (2... g6 {The King's Indian/Modern setup is another valid practical weapon, especially if you already know those structures.}  ) 3. e3 {The topical route.}  (3. c3  Qb6! {Punish the early bishop by attacking b2 and d4.} 4. Qb3  (4. Nd2  d5 5. e3 Nc6 {Black develops normally; the d4 pawn can become tender because c3 is not yet supporting it.}) Qxb3 5. axb3 cxd4 6. cxd4 Nc6 {Black is comfortable: the a-file and the d4 target matter more than the visual doubled pawn.} 7. e3 Nh5 {A recurring motif: harass the bishop and create imbalance.} 8. Bg5 h6 9. Bh4 g5 10. Bg3 Nxg3 11. hxg3 Bg7)  (3. dxc5  Na6! {This is not a scary pawn grab. Black uses the knight and queen to regain c5 with active play.} 4. Qd4 Qa5+ 5. c3 Qxc5! {Prefer queen recapture when ...Nxc5 would allow b4 tactics.} 6. Qxc5 Nxc5 7. Nd2 d6 8. Ngf3 e5 {Black has easy development and central space.})  (3. d5  d6! {Against the space-gaining d5, do not drift. Play for ...e5 and a reversed King's Indian/Benoni flavor.} 4. Nc3 e5! {Force White to commit. Black expects to solve the central tension later with ...d5.}  (4... g6 {Also playable if you want a pure King's Indian approach.}  ) 5. dxe6  (5. Bg5  Be7 6. e4? Nxd5! {A standard tactical detail: the knight on d5 also protects the bishop, so White cannot simply win a piece.} 7. exd5 Bxg5 {Black wins the bishop and emerges with comfortable play.})  (5. Bg3  h5! {A Stockfish-style but very practical space grab. The bishop can become a target.} 6. h3 h4 7. Bh2 b5! {Benko-style counterplay before White finishes development.} 8. Nxb5 Nbd7 {Black continues development and keeps the pressure rather than chasing one forcing trick.}) Bxe6 {The backward d-pawn is only temporary; Black will prepare ...d5.} 6. e4 Be7 7. Nf3 O-O 8. Be2 Nc6 9. O-O d5 {Black has equalized the structure and can enjoy the active dark-square game.}) cxd4! {Naroditsky likes taking before ...Qb6 because the structure is no longer symmetrical: White has a queenside majority, Black has central/kingside mass, and tactical chances appear.} 4. exd4 Qb6! {The queen attacks b2 and d4. This is the main drawback of the early London bishop: b2 lacks a natural defender.} 5. Nc3! {The principled answer. It protects b2 tactically and asks Black what the system is.}  (5. b3?! {Common, but strategically costly. White defends b2 by weakening c3, b4, and a3.}  d5! 6. Nf3 Nc6 7. Be2 Bf5 {Black develops with the queenside dark squares already softened.})  (5. Qc1?!  d5 6. Nf3 Nc6 {The queen is clumsy and the d4 pawn remains a long-term question.}) a6! {Underrated and practical. It covers b5 before playing ...d5, keeps White out of familiar London comfort, and pairs naturally with the later queenside expansion.}  (5... Qxb2?! {Naroditsky's warning: do not greedily grab b2 after Nc3.}  6. Nb5! {White gets dangerous initiative and too much theory. Practical repertoire rule: make White uncomfortable, do not enter their forcing prep.})  (5... d5?!  6. Nb5 Na6 {Playable, but awkward. The knight can be frozen on a6, which is exactly what ...a6 avoids.}) 6. Rb1 {Defends b2 without playing b3. White avoids dark-square weaknesses but spends a tempo.}  (6. a3  d5 7. Nf3 Bg4 {This is the cleanest equality try for White, but Black still gets the kind of game desired.} 8. Be2 e6 9. O-O Bd6 {An Exchange Caro-Kann style structure where Black is fully comfortable.}) d5! {Now ...a6 has done its job, so Black should play consistently in the center.} 7. Nf3?! {Natural but strategically loose because it allows the pin. The d4 pawn is the real target: the c-pawn cannot defend it while the knight sits on c3.}  (7. Be2  Nc6 8. Nf3 Bg4 {Still the same idea.}) Bg4! {Do not miss this. The F3 knight is the main defender of d4, so pin it before White can organize.} 8. Be2 Nc6 9. Be3?! {First serious concession: White retreats to overprotect d4 and lets Black improve naturally.}  (9. Ne5! {Best practical chance. White must challenge before Black completes development.}  Bxe2 10. Nxe2 e6 11. c3 Bd6 {Solid and probably equal, but Black is happy: the London player had to give up the autopilot setup.})  (9. O-O  ) e6! {Reinforce the center before any kingside fianchetto dreams. In this structure the dark bishop belongs on d6, much like an Exchange Caro-Kann.} 10. O-O Bd6 11. h3 Bh5! {Keep the bishop pair and keep tension. Do not take on f3 just because you can.} 12. a3 Qc7! {A key lesson: a piece can finish its job. The queen pressured b2/d4 from b6; now c7 stops b4 ideas, supports the c-file, protects e5, and clears b7-b5.} 13. Qd2 O-O 14. Kh1 b5! {Ambitious improvement. Black grabs space, clamps c4, and makes White search for a plan.} 15. b4?! {Panic. White weakens c4 and creates future targets.} Rfc8! {Put the rook where the play is. The queenside is the active board sector, and the c-file pressure now matters.} 16. Bd3 Bxf3! {Only now give up the bishop: it ruins White's kingside structure and helps the knight reach c4 later.} 17. gxf3 Nb8! {A beautiful retreating maneuver: ...Nb8-d7-b6-c4. The center is closed, so the extra tempo is less important than preserving the other knight on f6.}  (17... Nd7 {Simpler, but it moves the f6 knight away from its useful central/kingside role.}  ) 18. Rg1 g6! {Do not fear the ugly-looking dark-square cover. The pawn is often safer on g6 than g7 because it blunts Bh6/Qh6 ideas.} 19. Ne2 Nh5! {Knights on the rim can be excellent when they control the important squares. This knight restrains g3 and f4 and opens the queen's path toward h4.} 20. f4 Qd8! {The clean practical move. The queen swings to h4 and Black is now the side attacking the kingside because White's structure is damaged.} 21. Rg4 Nxf4! {Tactical conversion. If White recaptures, ...Qh4 creates a double threat.}  (21... Qh4! {Naroditsky thought this was the cleaner practical move: threaten mate first and keep maximum control.}  ) 22. Bxf4 {In the exact video analysis Naroditsky highlights an Nf5 defensive resource before the attack crashes through. In this compact model line the practical lesson is the same: check for forcing defensive resources before choosing the pretty tactic.} Qh4! {Double attack: ...Qxh3 mate ideas and the bishop on f4. White must solve mate threats and material loss at once.} *

